What Happens When You Type https://www.google.com and Press Enter?
This question is a staple in software engineering interviews and evaluates your understanding of web technologies. It’s a great opportunity to showcase your knowledge of the 
internet’s underlying mechanisms. Below, we’ll break down the steps that occur when you type a URL into your browser and press Enter.

DNS Request
The browser checks its cache for the IP address of www.google.com. If not found, it queries the operating system, which reaches out to DNS servers in a hierarchical 
process involving root servers, TLD servers, and authoritative DNS servers. Finally, the IP address is returned to the browser.

TCP/IP
With the IP address obtained, the browser establishes a TCP connection on port 443 using a three-way handshake. This ensures reliable communication between the browser 
and the server over the Internet Protocol (IP).

Firewall
Before the request reaches the server, it passes through firewalls that inspect the traffic for security threats. Firewalls use access control lists and other mechanisms 
to filter out malicious or unauthorized requests.

HTTPS/SSL
To secure the communication, the browser and server perform a TLS handshake. This process involves verifying the server’s SSL certificate and exchanging encryption 
keys to protect the data in transit.

Load Balancer
The request is routed through a load balancer, which distributes traffic across multiple servers. This ensures the system remains highly available and responsive, even under 
heavy load.

Web Server
The load balancer forwards the request to a web server, such as Nginx or Apache. The web server handles static content and forwards dynamic content requests to an 
application server.

Application Server
The application server executes backend logic and generates the required dynamic content. It might use frameworks like Django, Flask, or Node.js to process the request.

Database
If the request involves data retrieval, the application server queries the database. The database returns the necessary data, which the application server incorporates 
into the response.

Response Rendering
The web server sends the response back to the browser, which renders the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to display the webpage. At this point, you see www.google.com 
fully loaded in your browser.

Understanding these steps demonstrates how interconnected systems ensure smooth and secure web experiences. From DNS resolution to database queries, each layer is vital in 
delivering a seamless result.